This study investigates the simultaneous measurement of electrical brain activity and cerebral blood flow during a hand-grip task using electroencephalography (EEG) and diffuse correlation spectroscopy (DCS).

Movement was associated with event-related desynchronization of the EEG sensorimotor rhythm and increased cerebral blood flow measured over motor and frontal regions. The work explored whether combining electrical and optical measurements could provide a more complete characterization of movement-related brain dynamics and potentially expand command signals available to brain-computer interfaces.

